WEBVTT

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.350
Security levels express increasing degrees of protection for industrial automation and

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:05.360
control functions.

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.000
They provide a structured way to translate risk into security expectations.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.180
A higher level represents resistance to a more capable, motivated,

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:16.420
and well-resourced threat, but it also requires stronger capabilities, implementation,

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:17.240
and evidence.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:21.485
The target security level describes the protection required for a zone or conduit based on

00:00:21.485 --> 00:00:22.015
risk.

00:00:22.015 --> 00:00:26.590
It should result from analysis of threats, consequences, exposure, existing safeguards,

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:27.660
and risk tolerance.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.690
It is not selected simply because a system is important or because a supplier markets a

00:00:31.690 --> 00:00:32.275
high level.

00:00:32.275 --> 00:00:36.310
A component's capability security level describes the security capability it can support

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:38.105
when correctly configured and used.

00:00:38.105 --> 00:00:41.980
The achieved security level describes what the integrated and operated system actually

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:42.475
provides.

00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:45.130
These concepts are related but not interchangeable.

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:48.730
A capable device can be undermined by poor architecture, shared credentials,

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:50.800
weak remote access, or disabled features.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.440
Security level selection should be specific to the foundational requirements and the

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:55.400
system context.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:59.355
A zone may need particularly strong integrity and restricted data flow while having

00:00:59.355 --> 00:01:01.040
different confidentiality needs.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.320
Independent safety functions, physical controls,

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.645
and operational procedures also influence risk,

00:01:05.645 --> 00:01:08.150
but they should be documented rather than assumed.

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:09.455
Verification matters.

00:01:09.455 --> 00:01:13.070
Teams should be able to trace a target level to risk decisions, requirements,

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:16.710
design controls, component capabilities, configuration, test results,

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:17.955
and operational procedures.

00:01:17.955 --> 00:01:21.160
Changes to connectivity, process consequence, threat conditions,

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.075
or components may require reassessment.

00:01:23.075 --> 00:01:26.085
Security levels are therefore decision and engineering tools.

00:01:26.085 --> 00:01:28.765
They do not certify an entire facility with one number,

00:01:28.765 --> 00:01:32.480
and they do not guarantee that a system remains secure throughout its life.
